    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Memory impairment is a symptom of most neurodegenerative diseases. Yet restoring memory is impossible because we do not understand the molecular correlates underlying it. Currently, the field of neuroscience follows the hypothesis that the biological basis of memory lies in the ability of neural networks to physically change based on experience. Long-term potentiation (LTP), a form of synaptic strengthening that results from strong activation of a synapse, has been discussed as a potential facilitator of memory formation and retrieval. LTP utilizes SNARE complexes containing Syntaxin-3 to increase neurotransmitter trafficking at the postsynaptic membrane, resulting in increased postsynaptic AMPA receptors. LTP has been studied for decades, but a causal link between LTP and memory has been difficult to establish due to the difficulty of deleting LTP without altering other neuronal activities. The present study uses a conditional knockout of Syntaxin-3 in mice to demonstrate that Syntaxin-3 is necessary for LTP and to test the role of LTP in memory. We found that Syntaxin-3 deletion in the hippocampus failed to result in significant differences in performance on memory tasks. Voltage-clamp electrophysiological recordings from hippocampal slices confirmed that LTP was selectively eliminated by the Syntaxin-3 deletion. Evaluation of short-term memory tasks (object location task, novel object recognition task) as well as long-term memory tasks (Barnes maze, fear conditioning) showed that knockout mice and control mice performed similarly without a statistically significant difference. The results of this study indicate that hippocampal LTP may not be required for memory storage and retrieval in these short-term and long-term memory tasks. However, the ability to selectively affect LTP without altering other forms of neuronal activity makes the Syntaxin-3 conditional knockout mouse a key tool for future studies of long-term potentiation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
